  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss, so it is 4 times steeper left-right. Steps grow without limit left-right once learning rate > 0.5, so 1.0 diverges and 0.1 settles.",
    "Each step moves by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise sd = 2.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)): largest at batch 1, exactly zero at 64.",
    "Orange arrow: the actual next step (against the noisy slope). Grey arrow: −learning rate × true slope. Both use the same scale, 1 bowl unit per unit of step length.",
    "One update every 6 host ticks (5 per second at 1× speed). A run ends after 40 steps, or as 'diverged' once loss > 12. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence.",
    "The ball is drawn no higher than the top of the bowl. The loss readout always shows the true value. Each trail keeps at most 40 landing points."
  ]
}